---
// origin: human. Written by Ken, not the pipeline. Static page by design (see /writing/).
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import JsonLd from '../../../components/JsonLd.astro';
import { essayBySlug, authorshipOf } from '../../../data/writing';
import { personKenAshe, websiteKenAshe } from '../../../data/schema';
import { SITE_URL } from '../../../consts';
import EssayFigure from '../../../components/EssayFigure.astro';
import { ogImageOf } from '../../../data/writing-images';

const essay = essayBySlug('ai-editor-trusted-least');
const authorship = authorshipOf(essay);

const title = 'I Used AI to Make My First Video. The AI Editor Was the Part I Trusted Least.';
const description =
  'What making a first talking-head video with AI tools taught me: AI was great at scripting, captions, and audio cleanup, and least trustworthy when it made editorial decisions.';
// Social/schema image: a 1200px derivative of the essay's declared image (writing.ts).
const og = await ogImageOf(essay);

// Article node for this essay. Author and publisher are the canonical Person (imported, not
// forked: DECISIONS.md D9); the essay is human-directed, so unlike Digest posts the author
// is Ken Ashe himself. Same self-contained @graph pattern as every other content page.
const pageUrl = new URL(`/writing/ai-editor-trusted-least/`, SITE_URL).toString();
const articleSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    {
      '@type': 'Article',
      '@id': `${pageUrl}#article`,
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      url: pageUrl,
      mainEntityOfPage: pageUrl,
      ...(og ? { image: new URL(og.src, SITE_URL).toString() } : {}),
      datePublished: essay.published,
      author: { '@id': 'https://kenashe.ai/#ken-ashe' },
      publisher: { '@id': 'https://kenashe.ai/#ken-ashe' },
      isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://kenashe.ai/#website' },
      inLanguage: 'en-US',
    },
    websiteKenAshe,
    personKenAshe,
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  image={og?.src}
  ogImageWidth={og?.width}
  ogImageHeight={og?.height}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Writing', path: '/writing/' },
    { name: title, path: '/writing/ai-editor-trusted-least/' },
  ]}
>
  <JsonLd slot="jsonld" data={articleSchema} />
  <article class="essay">
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/writing/"><span aria-hidden="true">←</span> Writing</a>
    </nav>

    <header class="essay-header">
      <p class="page-mark" aria-hidden="true">// writing</p>
      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <p class="meta">
        <span class="meta-key">By</span> Ken Ashe
        <span aria-hidden="true">·</span> September 25, 2026
        <span aria-hidden="true">·</span> {authorship}
      </p>
    </header>

    <EssayFigure essay={essay} priority />

    <div class="prose">
      <p>I recently made my first published talking-head video.</p>
      <p>That sentence makes it sound much simpler than it was.</p>
      <p>I had an iPhone, a DJI gimbal, a microphone, a reasonably clear idea of what I wanted to say, and access to more AI tools than I could possibly need.</p>
      <p>I assumed AI would make the production part easy.</p>
      <p>In some ways, it did.</p>
      <p>In other ways, the most “AI” parts of the editing process were the parts I trusted least.</p>
      <p>That ended up being the useful lesson.</p>
      <p>The finished video is <a href="https://youtube.com/shorts/iEjd1OvHLS0" target="_blank" rel="noopener">How to Start Learning AI (Without Getting Overwhelmed)</a>.</p>
      <h2>The first attempt was mostly me learning what not to do</h2>
      <p>My original idea was to go outside, walk around and talk naturally into the camera.</p>
      <p>I had a general idea of what I wanted to say, but no real script. I figured I could wing it.</p>
      <p>I could not.</p>
      <p>I wandered. I looked at notes. There was too much background noise. I forgot to use the external microphone. The location looked good, but it was near a busy road.</p>
      <p>None of this was catastrophic. It was actually useful.</p>
      <p>The first recording taught me that I did not need more equipment. I needed a better process.</p>
      <p>The next version was simpler: stationary camera, quieter room, notes close to the lens, a real script, and shorter sections I could record separately.</p>
      <p>That immediately made things better.</p>
      <h2>AI was very good at helping me figure out what to say</h2>
      <p>The video was about a question I keep getting from people in corporate jobs and from small-business owners: how should I start learning AI?</p>
      <p>They are not ignoring it. They know it matters. They are just overwhelmed by the number of models, agents, coding tools, automations and new products appearing every week.</p>
      <p>The advice I wanted to give was simple.</p>
      <p>Don’t try to learn all of AI.</p>
      <p>Use AI for something you already do.</p>
      <p>Then use AI itself to teach you more about AI.</p>
      <p>AI was genuinely useful here.</p>
      <p>I used ChatGPT to help me turn the idea into a script, pressure-test the structure, shorten sections, think about the hook, and decide what belonged in this video versus a future one.</p>
      <p>That felt like an ideal use of AI.</p>
      <p>I still made the editorial decisions. But AI made it much faster to see the options.</p>
      <h2>Then I tried to let AI edit the video</h2>
      <p>This is where things got more interesting.</p>
      <p>I tried Descript first.</p>
      <p>The promise is compelling: upload the footage, transcribe it, edit the video by editing the text, remove filler words, clean up the audio, create captions and let AI help assemble the final piece.</p>
      <p>Some of that worked.</p>
      <p>But basic things I expected to be easy felt less intuitive than I expected: deciding exactly where a clip should start and stop, moving between takes, and making small deterministic edits.</p>
      <p>Then I tried CapCut.</p>
      <p>CapCut’s conventional timeline immediately made more sense to me. I could see the clips, trim them, arrange them and understand what was happening.</p>
      <p>Then I tried its AI features.</p>
      <p>One CapCut workflow treated my footage as raw material for a generated video and started producing another person instead of editing me.</p>
      <p>That was not what I had in mind.</p>
      <p>Another used the real footage, then cut pauses, repetitions and what it decided was redundant.</p>
      <p>Technically, it succeeded.</p>
      <p>It made the video shorter.</p>
      <p>It also removed enough context that parts of my argument stopped making sense.</p>
      <p>I undid both.</p>
      <h2>The boring AI features were the most useful</h2>
      <p>The AI features I ended up liking were much less dramatic.</p>
      <p>Automatic captions were useful.</p>
      <p>Filler-word removal was useful.</p>
      <p>Volume normalization across several separately recorded clips was useful.</p>
      <p>Transcription was useful.</p>
      <p>Having AI identify possible pauses and rough spots was useful.</p>
      <p>Those are not the features that make the best demo video.</p>
      <p>They are the features that actually saved me time.</p>
      <p>The more subjective the decision became, the more I wanted to make it myself. Is this sentence redundant? Does this idea still make sense without the previous sentence?</p>
      <h2>AI worked best as an assistant, not an editor-in-chief</h2>
      <p>I ended up with a workflow that was much more human than I expected.</p>
      <p>I decided what I wanted to say.</p>
      <p>AI helped me structure it.</p>
      <p>I recorded it.</p>
      <p>I chose the takes.</p>
      <p>AI helped remove filler words, normalize the audio and generate captions.</p>
      <p>I chose what stayed and what went.</p>
      <p>Then I published the finished video as a YouTube Short, and used it on LinkedIn and X.</p>
      <p>The final product absolutely benefited from AI.</p>
      <p>But AI did not make the video for me.</p>
      <p>There is a temptation right now to assume that the most advanced workflow is the one where AI makes the most decisions.</p>
      <p>My experience was almost the opposite.</p>
      <p>For this project, the best workflow was the one where AI handled the repetitive parts and left the consequential judgment to me.</p>
      <p>The useful question is not “Can AI do this?”</p>
      <p>It probably can.</p>
      <p>The more useful question is:</p>
      <p>Should AI do this part?</p>
      <p>For captions? Sure.</p>
      <p>For cleaning up inconsistent volume? Absolutely.</p>
      <p>For suggesting a better hook? Very useful.</p>
      <p>For deciding which part of my argument is unnecessary?</p>
      <p>I want to review that decision.</p>
      <p>For replacing me with a generated person?</p>
      <p>Definitely not.</p>
      <p>This was only one video, so I am not claiming to have solved AI-assisted video production.</p>
      <p>I am still learning the tools.</p>
      <h2>What I will do next time</h2>
      <p>Next time I will write the script first, record indoors in short sections, and choose the takes myself.</p>
      <p>AI can pressure-test the script, then handle transcription, filler words, captions and volume.</p>
      <p>I will not ask an editor to assemble the video or generate a person.</p>
      <p>If an AI cut removes a sentence, I will read the argument without that sentence before I accept it.</p>
      <p>That is the broader lesson.</p>
      <p>You do not need to automate an entire creative process to get value from AI.</p>
      <p>Sometimes the best use of AI is much less exciting:</p>
      <p>Let it handle the tedious parts.</p>
      <p>Keep the judgment.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .essay {
    max-width: 44rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 4rem 1.5rem 0;
  }
  .crumbs {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin: 0 0 2.5rem;
  }
  .crumbs a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color 150ms ease;
  }
  .crumbs a:hover {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .page-mark {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--accent);
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }
  .essay-header h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem);
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    line-height: 1.08;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  .meta {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin: 0 0 2.5rem;
  }
  .meta-key {
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
  }
  .meta span[aria-hidden] {
    color: var(--accent);
    padding: 0 0.35rem;
  }
  .prose {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  .prose p {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
  }
  .prose a {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .prose h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 3vw, 1.75rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    line-height: 1.2;
    margin: 2.5rem 0 0.9rem;
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  .prose ul {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    padding-left: 1.4rem;
  }
  .prose li {
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }
  .prose li::marker {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .prose blockquote {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    padding: 0.2rem 0 0.2rem 1.1rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .prose blockquote p {
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }
  .prose blockquote p:last-child {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .prose strong {
    color: var(--fg);
    font-weight: 650;
  }
</style>
